], "calls": [["Glob", "**/query.sql"], ["Read", "/private/tmp/wk/207478fba8/workbench/query.sql"], ["Bash", "git log --oneline -10 && ls -a && grep -rniE \"create (unique )?index|orders\" --include=*.sql --include=*.py --include=*.js --include=*.ts --include=*.rb -l . 2>/dev/null | head -20"], ["Bash", "cat README.md | head -40; echo ---; grep -niE \"slow|orders|query|index|migrat\" app.log | head -20; echo ---; head -20 commits.txt"]], "final_message": "The query is a likely cause, but I can't confirm it from here. The workbench has no schema, no `EXPLAIN` output and no migration history. All I have is the single statement.\n\n**The likely problem is `lower(u.email) = '...'`.**\n- Wrapping the column in `lower()` stops the database from using a plain index on `users.email`. It has to scan the whole `users` table, lowercase every row, and then join to `orders`.\n- That scan gets slower as the table grows. It would fit a slowdown that crept in \"since last week\", for example if signups crossed a size threshold or the planner changed its plan after a stats refresh.\n- It would also fit if an index on `email` used to be used and a change broke that. One example is a code change that added `lower()` to a lookup that used to be an exact match.\n\n**Fixes, in order of preference:**\n1. **Add an expression index** (Postgres syntax):\n   ```sql\n   CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n   ```\n   The query then works unchanged.\n2. **Or store emails lowercased and drop the `lower()`.** Normalize on write and compare directly, with a unique index on `email`. Alternatively, use a `citext` column in Postgres or a case-insensitive collation in MySQL.\n3. **Check `orders.user_id` has an index.** Without one, the join is a second full scan. It's the other usual cause of this pattern.\n\n**Smaller point:** `SELECT *` over a join pulls every column from both tables, including whatever is in `users`. Select only the columns the page renders.\n\n**To confirm, run this on the slow database:**\n```sql\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT * FROM orders o JOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id WHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\nIf you see `Seq Scan on users` with a large \"Rows Removed by Filter\", that's the cause. If `users` is fast and the time goes into `orders`, the problem is the `user_id` index.\n\n**If it still isn't the query:** 8 seconds for a single-email lookup is a lot even for a seq scan, unless `users` has millions of rows. The orders page may be running this query many times (an N+1), or the slowdown may be elsewhere in the request. `app.log` only has a \"slow disk\" warning, which isn't enough to say.\n\nIf you paste the `EXPLAIN` output and the table definitions, I can say for certain. I haven't changed anything.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}